WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.224
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.724 --> 00:00:05.250
GARY GENSLER: We
can use technology

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.010
to make finance more
inclusive and more

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.520
accessible to many more millions
of people in our country

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.050
and many more
hundreds of millions

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.455
of people around the globe.

00:00:16.455 --> 00:00:20.420
SARAH HANSEN: Today on
Chalk Radio, modern money.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.600
In this episode, we're taking
a look at 15.S08 FinTech:

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.730
Shaping the Financial World.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.590
This is a course,
about money and how

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.280
it works in a constantly
changing digital environment.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.020
GARY GENSLER: My
research and teaching

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.390
is all about the intersection
between finance and technology.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.996
And what better place
to do it but at MIT?

00:00:42.996 --> 00:00:46.260
SARAH HANSEN: Our guest today
is Professor Gary Gensler.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.320
He co-directs MIT's
FinTech@CSAIL,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.040
which stands for Computer
Science and AI Lab.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.190
Professor Gensler has
a unique perspective

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.240
in that he's teaching a subject
that's continuously evolving.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.640
So how does an educator
teach something that

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.220
changes so much and so rapidly?

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.020
How do you prepare
students to work

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.300
in a new and, oftentimes,
vastly different

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.400
context, one that literally
might not even exist yet?

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.380
But before we dive
into all of that,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.780
we first have to
understand, what is FinTech?

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.780
GARY GENSLER: This
term, FinTech,

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.970
which came about in the
last 20 or so years,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.300
can mean different things
to different folks.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.650
For me, I think of it as
how is finance changing

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.180
due to new technologies?

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.610
And the original
FinTech was money

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.210
itself invented thousands
of years ago by humans.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.420
But usually, a lot
of folks these days

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.810
will call FinTech
the companies that

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.870
are disrupting the world
of finance using machine

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:56.730
learning or using something
called blockchain technology.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.970
But 20 years ago, it
would be correct to say

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.630
that what technology was
disrupting finance more

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.410
than anything else
was the internet.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.960
And 10 or 15 years
ago, the technology

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.870
that was disrupting
finance the most

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.930
was mobile phones and cloud.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.710
And so I teach it
from a broader view

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:22.070
and try to prepare
students with this concept,

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.980
so they're ready for
the 2030s and the 2040s.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.760
What technologies then will be
highly disruptive in finance?

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.300
It's really about how
technology is changing

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.090
the world of finance
and has for centuries

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.730
and will for centuries to come.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.200
And right now,
we're living through

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.980
this transitional period
of machine learning,

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.670
artificial intelligence,
Internet of Things,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.510
blockchain technology.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.160
In a decade from now, it will
be some other transition.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.040
And so I really try
to engage the students

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.910
on what that means, what those
big technology movements mean

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.540
as they come into
the technology stack,

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.330
and then they just
become accepted.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.194
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:08.058 --> 00:03:10.450
SARAH HANSEN: In helping
me understand what FinTech

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.810
actually is, Professor Gensler
made some really interesting

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.690
observations about the
way we use money now

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.900
versus even just 10 years
ago and what that implies

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.420
about where we're headed.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.050
GARY GENSLER: That
which we call money,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.360
that which Plato and
Aristotle called money,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.110
was just society's
representation of something

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.980
of value.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.220
But boy, in the 21st
century, aren't we there--

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.130
that you can just wave
your mobile phone,

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.840
and you move value--

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.430
what we call money.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.670
The greatest changes
of the early 2020s

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:46.890
is about two forms of
artificial intelligence.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.620
One is machine learning.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.520
Machine learning has
a remarkable ability

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.930
to extract correlations
in data where

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.890
we couldn't find it earlier
with earlier forms of math--

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.080
linear algebra and so forth.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.380
And the second form of
artificial intelligence

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.870
that's dramatically
changing finance

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.030
is natural language processing.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.170
What do these words mean?

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.570
They're just kind of
fancy words to say,

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:16.100
can you move human language
to computer language?

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.630
And guess what?

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.670
All day long, that's
happening, and we're

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.790
starting to get robo advice.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:28.730
And robo advisory platforms
can deliver finance far more

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.970
broadly to far more people.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.800
So Bank of America has its
voice assistant, Erica.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.280
But millions of their
customers are now using Erica--

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.000
[LAUGHS]---- to do
modest transactions--

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.250
move bank balances
around, things like that.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:51.410
So we try, in the classroom, to
think about this with students

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.730
and sort of prepare them.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.230
What are the next technologies?

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.630
Not just the technologies
like machine learning

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.400
and natural language
processing, but what

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.110
are the next technologies?

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.470
And how do we think of
entrepreneurial strategy--

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.090
how to think of the strategy to
use new technologies to provide

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.440
services.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.080
And then the classic
way that you always

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.450
have to think about
as an entrepreneur--

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:17.960
how can you do something better,
quicker, cheaper that's not

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.366
been done today?

00:05:20.366 --> 00:05:22.198
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:23.572 --> 00:05:25.290
SARAH HANSEN: During
my conversation

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.930
with Professor Gensler,
I began to realize

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.130
how far-reaching the
implications of FinTech can be.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.470
Expanding access to
financial literacy

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.890
is a really important outcome
that drives his teaching

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.615
and his passion for the work.

00:05:39.615 --> 00:05:41.610
GARY GENSLER: What
I was drawn to MIT

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:46.140
about was this sense that maybe
we could democratize capitalism

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:51.420
a bit more, that we
can use technology

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.180
to make finance more
inclusive and more

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.720
accessible to many more millions
of people in our country

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.220
and many more
hundreds of millions

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.230
of people around the globe.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:05.190
New technologies come
along every decade or so,

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.610
and then they disrupt
the status quo.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.740
And what's really
exciting is, at MIT,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.200
we're at the forefront of
those new technologies.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.300
And we can bring those
practices, those new practices,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.920
into the classroom
and say, well, now

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.780
what's this doing to finance?

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.760
What does it mean that a big
technology company is starting

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.160
to have a credit card, maybe?

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.460
What does it mean that Apple
credit card exists, or Facebook

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.210
wants to have a worldwide
currency called Libra

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:40.740
or that we might
provide insurance that's

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.940
going to be
measuring our Fitbits

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:49.980
or watches or our mobile devices
as to whether we're healthy

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.681
or not?

00:06:51.681 --> 00:06:55.080
SARAH HANSEN: Students from
all areas of MIT's Sloan School

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.660
of Management, and even
students registered

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.840
from other schools, flock
to Professor Gensler's class

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.920
to learn more about what the
future of finance looks like

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.490
and how they might
help shape it.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.460
GARY GENSLER: Students
come into this class

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.960
for multiple purposes.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:16.110
But I'd say the majority
are really interested how

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.440
these new technologies
might be disrupting

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.510
a big part of an economy.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.390
And the students are
from all over the globe.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:28.580
And dominantly,
they're thinking about,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.470
I want to understand
how this technology is

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.080
disrupting finance.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:39.140
So I asked all the students to
write a memo, a clearly worded

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.180
recommendation memo.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.200
But their recommendation-- they
can pick one of three CEOs--

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.040
Jeff Bezos of Amazon;
Brian Moynihan,

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.050
who runs Bank of America;
or Mark Andreessen,

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.838
who runs a big venture capital
firm, Andreessen Horowitz.

00:07:55.838 --> 00:07:57.380
And I said, you pick
whether you want

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:00.500
to write about the
provision of credit,

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.470
the provision of credit cards,
the provision of automobile

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.270
insurance, the
provision of mortgages.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:09.410
You pick whatever you
want to pick in finance,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.260
but you have to write
a CEO recommendation

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.590
memo with a clearly stated
recommendation to Amazon,

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.560
Bank of America, or
Andreessen Horowitz.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.290
And a little over half the
students picked Amazon.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.530
They were all really
interesting memos to read.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:26.330
And the students engage.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.480
They engage because
there's tremendous benefit

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.340
to giving students a real
live thing to address.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.190
It's a real live thing
where I say, listen,

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.220
if this is a great memo,
I'll figure out the way

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.030
to get it to Jeff Bezos.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.390
I'll figure out the way to
get it to Brian Moynihan.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:47.980
And the students believe
me because I could get

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.000
a really great memo to them.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Some students will say, can I
write it to somebody in Brazil

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:53.500
instead?

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.230
And I always say yes.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:09:01.820
Had a great memo to the CEO
of Tencent, the big technology

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.440
network in China.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.140
And it was one of the best
memos this group of students

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.400
put together.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.270
I would say to other
educators, trust the students.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:16.625
If you give them a little bit of
an open-ended prompt like this,

00:09:16.625 --> 00:09:19.450
a big majority will
rise to the occasion.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.580
SARAH HANSEN: As is the case
with most things nowadays,

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.730
Professor Gensler's course is
formatted a little differently

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.790
in the time of COVID-19.

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.260
But he's seen some
pretty remarkable results

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:32.930
at achieving some
semblance of normalcy

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.580
in his new class format.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.800
GARY GENSLER: We're in
very unusual times--

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.770
that those of us who
learned our teaching

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.650
skills in the
classroom with anywhere

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.820
from 15 to 100
students in the class,

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:51.540
that you are all having
a shared experience.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.870
All of a sudden, with a snap
of a finger, we're online.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:00.950
Most of us at Sloan felt it
was really important to keep

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:02.700
some sense of community.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:08.330
And so when I started
the first class online,

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:11.480
I asked all the students--

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:18.170
those that could-- to listen
together synchronously.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:22.070
And so this class was
taught at 8:30 to 10:00 AM

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:24.420
on Monday and Wednesday.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.750
And so for many time
zones, it worked.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.760
But did it really work
for somebody sitting

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.880
in Europe who had flown back?

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.290
So we did record it, but
we got about 85% to 90%

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:39.260
of the students tying
in synchronously.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.330
And I felt it was
not only important

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:44.360
that it was synchronous,
but secondly, that everyone

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:46.898
had their video on.

00:10:46.898 --> 00:10:48.190
And I would tell the students--

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.570
I said, I know
that you might have

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:57.050
a friend, a spouse, a lover, a
child walk through the camera.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:58.910
You might have a
delivery, which--

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.790
One day, I was teaching,
and I had a delivery.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.770
And there was a
pounding on the door.

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.380
And I had to say to the
students, excuse me.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.480
I have to go to the door.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.930
But I said, that's life.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.490
We're going to relax
about all of this.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:18.398
But I felt it formed a
better sense of community.

00:11:18.398 --> 00:11:20.510
SARAH HANSEN: Before
teaching at MIT,

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:22.940
Professor Gensler cut his
teeth in the professional

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.800
and political world
working for Goldman Sachs;

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.280
the US Department of Treasury;
Hillary Clinton's campaign;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.030
Paul Sarbanes in the US
Senate, and with Barack Obama's

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:34.940
administration, chairing
something called the Commodity

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.010
Futures Trading Commission.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:40.430
His understanding of what makes
a successful, well-equipped

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.670
professional shines
through in his classes.

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:46.220
Gensler isn't just
developing domain knowledge

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.180
but cultivating humans
and professionals.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.770
I want to read something
to you that you've written.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:53.770
GARY GENSLER: Oh my God.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.220
This is the embarrassing moment.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:58.330
SARAH HANSEN: [LAUGHS]
No, it's good.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:02.560
It says "respect and courtesy
build reputations, networks,

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.210
and trust."

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.920
That seems important
in your teaching.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.820
So I'm wondering how your
focus on helping students

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:14.030
cultivate professionalism kind
of plays out in the course.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.070
How do you teach that?

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:18.430
GARY GENSLER: So
I think that each

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:23.950
of us that walk into a classroom
have a big responsibility.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:25.960
It's such a privilege
to be at MIT

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:30.040
with this incredible student
body and faculty and staff.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.530
But I feel that we have this
responsibility that goes

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.710
beyond our domain knowledge.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:39.200
Of course, I'm in the classroom
teaching finance and technology

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:43.210
and this intersection of how
machine learning and blockchain

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.580
technology and
Internet of Things

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:48.340
is changing the
world of finance.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.470
But I also think
that when I look back

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.770
at the faculty members I learned
the most from when I went--

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:55.300
I went to Wharton--

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.310
it was not about
the domain knowledge

00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:02.090
of accounting or finance or
linear algebra or biology.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:04.090
It was more about values.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.990
It was more about
professionalism.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:10.960
And I've been honored to
work at very high levels

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:12.940
of professionalism.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.670
Often, the race
goes to the person

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:19.240
who's prepped the most,
who's been courteous, who

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:24.850
listens as much as they
talk, who asks questions

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:26.740
that are relevant.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:30.770
And I come back to that,
not just in the classroom,

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:33.740
but I offer lunches
to the students.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.520
They sign up in an
open signup forum

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:39.400
where up to eight
students can sign up.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.050
And I usually do this in person.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.120
But what I found
in this COVID time,

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.690
there was such a demand that
I set up lunches twice a week

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:51.880
all through the six weeks.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:56.150
And they were nearly always
booked with eight students.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:58.540
And we would just
have no agenda other

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:01.600
than we would talk about
careers and professionalism

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.170
and the like.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:06.520
So maybe for some small
percentage of the students,

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:08.710
it will stick.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.090
And then I usually
end my last lecture

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.820
in every one of my
classes, and I do a quote

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:16.650
from Benjamin Franklin.

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:19.820
And Benjamin Franklin, believe
it or not, spoke about money.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:24.840
So I figure I get a
little bit of a pass

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:27.750
if I get hokey on the last day.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:31.860
And I bring Benjamin Franklin
and this wonderful, warm quote

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:35.340
that you can look up about
Benjamin Franklin and money.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:38.340
But what he really is talking
about is paying it forward.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:41.610
He talks about if you should--

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.470
somebody does you a favor.

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:48.020
The way that you repay the favor
is doing the favor to others.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:52.290
And he says this is the best
form of money he knows--

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.190
to redo a favor by
paying it back to others.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:59.250
I feel that those of
us who are privileged

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:02.010
to be in these positions,
if we can share that,

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:03.840
and it sticks with
some percentage

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:06.750
of the students, that's
as important-- maybe

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:11.430
more important-- than the
domain knowledge and the domain

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:12.360
analysis.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.330
I mean, I like to think we
teach a lot more than knowledge,

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.730
that we teach a way of thinking.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.406
